HOW TO MODIFY AND CUSTOMIZE EXAM TITLES IN THE 'All Available Exams' JLIST -------------------

Most likely the exam creator/professor will want to customize the 'All Available Exams' list. The default settings
list exams as Exam 1, Exam 2, Exam 3, etc., and you might want to change that to something like
Exam 1 - Doctrine of the Trinity. The following explains how to make these changes, and describes
the relevant classes and methods related thereto.

When the Builder app is first loaded it displays default settings of list exams such as Exam 1, Exam 2, Exam 3, etc.
It accomplishes this by instantiating class LoadInitExamLabsJList, and calls rowCountExamsLab() to retrieve a row count from the 
EXAMS_JLIST_LABELS of table which stores a serialized instance of ExamLabelsJlist1, a class with setters and getters for String
values representing the exam labels. If one such object does not exist an instance of LoadInitExamLabsJList calls loadJListExamLabs() and
inserts into the db a 'placeholder' object of type ExamLabelsJlist1, a copy of which is immeditely returned 
with loadInitExamLabsJList.getExamLabelsJlist1(). That returned ExamLabelsJlist1 is used to capture the default Strings
such as getExam1Str() when the app first opens, and assigns that to String variables which the JList displays as, ie. Exam 1.

To alter these exam titles click 'Exam Labels' where you will be presented with a JOptionPane holding
ten labels with corresponding JTextField rows. Make your changes to one or more of these text fields and
select 'Yes" to apply those changes. Programmatically, Java uses examLabelsJlist1 (a reference to an instance of the ExamLabelsJlist1 class) to set 
and get String values representing  exam labels. Notice that within those setters an instance of ExamLabelsJlist1 is passed in the call to 
loadInitExamLabsJList.setExamLabelsJlist1(this), which allows the first JList and the JOptionPane to reflect those changes. 

Unfortunately, after those changes have been made you will be informed that to see those changes take effect the app must close, 
requiring you to load it up once again. It is possible to perform a class load of some kind to programmatically
reflect those changes in real time without this additional step, but I'm still working on that. 

